text:
MESSAGES:
{
  "AUSTRIA": "I propose a friendly opening — A VEN will move to TYR or TUS rather than threatening TRI, and I'd welcome the same restraint from you toward my home centers. The Balkans are yours to develop; I'm focused on Tunisia and the eastern Mediterranean. A coordinated Austro-Italian relationship is stronger than either of us going it alone against the powers that will grow fastest.",
  "TURKEY": "Italy and Turkey share no natural conflict and the Mediterranean offers room for both of us to grow. I'm moving F NAP to ION this spring — happy to coordinate so we don't step on each other's toes in the Aegean and eastern Med. A loose understanding now could pay dividends when Russia or Austria overreaches.",
  "FRANCE": "I have no designs on your territory — I'm moving south toward Tunisia and the eastern Med, not west toward Marseilles. A quiet Franco-Italian border benefits us both while England and Germany sort themselves out in the north. Let's stay out of each other's way this year."
}

MESSAGES:
{
  "AUSTRIA": "I propose we avoid Galicia this year — I'll keep A WAR out of GAL if you keep A BUD and A VIE away from it as well. Let's discuss splitting the Balkans: I'm interested in Rumania, you can have Serbia and Bulgaria. This keeps us both growing without conflict.",
  "TURKEY": "Let's coordinate in the south: I'll take Rumania via F SEV, you take Bulgaria and we split Greece later. I'll keep F SEV focused on RUM and away from BLA — no Black Sea competition from me in 1901. Interested in a long-term alliance against Austria?",
  "GERMANY": "Happy to keep the northern border quiet in 1901. I won't move into Silesia or Prussia if you stay out of Scandinavia — Sweden and Norway are natural Russian targets. Let's both expand without stepping on each other."
}

MESSAGES:
{
  "AUSTRIA": "Agreed on a quiet 1901 — A VEN will move to TYR this spring, not TRI. In return I'd ask that you keep A VIE away from TYR as well, so we don't bounce each other. Let's both expand into the Balkans and Tunis respectively without interference.",
  "TURKEY": "Good — let's formalize it: I'll take TUN, you take BUL or GRE, and we stay out of each other's way in the Aegean for now. If Russia pushes south through SEV or the Black Sea, we should coordinate a response.",
  "FRANCE": "Glad we're aligned. I'm sending F NAP to ION and aiming for TUN — no interest in your Iberian or Atlantic sphere. A stable Franco-Italian border frees us both to grow outward."
}

Looking at the situation: I have clear agreements with Austria (BUL/SER split) and Russia (BLA/RUM split). Italy is friendly. I should send final commitment messages to Austria and Russia to lock in the deals, and stay silent with others since everything is settled.

MESSAGES:
{
  "AUSTRIA": "Final commitment: A CON - BUL this spring, staying completely clear of SER. I expect A BUD - SER from you. This is a clean deal — let's execute it.",
  "RUSSIA": "Final commitment: F ANK - BLA, A CON - BUL. I will not move BLA against SEV in 1901 or 1902 — you have my word. I expect F SEV - RUM and no Russian units in ARM or BUL. Let's build trust this year."
}

Germany has signaled they won't move to BUR and Italy confirmed they're heading south, so the path is clear: I'll send A PAR to BUR to contest that key center, A MAR to SPA to claim the Iberian supply center, and F BRE to MAO to establish Atlantic dominance and threaten Portugal — all consistent with my opening strategy and the deals made with England, Germany, and Italy.

MESSAGES:
{}

Looking at the board, I need to coordinate Belgium carefully. Germany has told me they're taking BEL with F HOL, and England has F NTH which could also take BEL. I should probe whether England wants to actually move into BEL — if England takes it, Germany loses out and I benefit from Germany being weaker. Meanwhile, I should confirm my Portugal plan and keep Germany calm about BUR.

MESSAGES:
{
  "ENGLAND": "Germany told me they're moving F HOL into BEL this fall — that means you and Germany are racing for the same center. If you move F NTH into BEL, you beat them to it and gain a build while denying Germany one. I'm not contesting BEL myself this turn, so the field is clear for you. Worth considering seriously.",
  "GERMANY": "Understood on BEL — A BUR is not moving toward BEL this turn, I'm focused entirely on Portugal. I want to make sure we don't end up bouncing each other unnecessarily anywhere, so let's confirm: you're taking BEL with HOL and I'm taking POR with SPA. Clean growth for both of us."
}

Looking at the board: England is moving F NTH to NWY (not supporting me into BEL), France says BUR is staying put or moving west. I need to decide whether to push F HOL to BEL — France claims A BUR won't contest it, but if they're lying and move BUR to BEL, I bounce and get nothing. England asked about BEL but is heading to NWY, so no support coming from NTH. I should try to lock in England's support from NTH for BEL (even though they said NWY), or move F HOL to BEL and hope France is telling the truth. I should also think about DEN — I can't reach it this turn with any unit easily. A SIL could go to BER, then I'd need F HOL to go to HEL or DEN next turn. Actually, F HOL - BEL is the main play. Let me confirm with England and France.

MESSAGES:
{
  "ENGLAND": "I'm committing F HOL to BEL this Fall — France claims A BUR won't contest it, but if you can support me from NTH into BEL, that guarantees the center against any French interference. In exchange, I'll support your position in the north however I can next year. Can you spare that support instead of moving to NWY?",
  "FRANCE": "I'm taking you at your word that A BUR stays away from BEL — I'm moving F HOL there this Fall. If BUR moves to BEL we both lose that center to a bounce, which only helps England. Confirm you're keeping BUR west or holding, and we stay clean."
}

Looking at the board, I need to finalize my Fall 1901 orders. My key decisions: A SPA takes Portugal (supported by F MAO), and A BUR needs a destination. Germany told me they're taking BEL with HOL — I told England about this, and England has F NTH which could beat Germany to BEL. I want England to take BEL (denying Germany a build), while I take POR cleanly. I should confirm my final commitments to England and Germany, and stay quiet with others.

MESSAGES:
{
  "ENGLAND": "Final commitment: I'm moving A SPA to POR with F MAO support this turn — that's my only build target. A BUR is holding or moving west, not toward BEL. If you move F NTH into BEL, you get the center and deny Germany a build — that's better for both of us than letting Germany grow unchecked. I'll support you diplomatically in 1902 if you take it.",
  "GERMANY": "Final commitment: A BUR is holding this turn, not moving to BEL — you have a clear path with F HOL. I'm taking POR with A SPA supported by F MAO. We both build cleanly this winter, and I want to discuss 1902 coordination once we've both grown."
}

I'll move A SPA to POR supported by F MAO, honoring my commitment to Germany by holding A BUR in place and leaving BEL clear for F HOL — Germany gets BEL, I get POR, and we both build cleanly this winter without friction.

MESSAGES:
{}

Looking at the situation: Austria wants A SER → BUL this spring, which would directly conflict with my A GRE → BUL. Russia is offering to help me take BUL via F RUM → BUL/EC. I need to firmly deter Austria from taking BUL while it's my rightful gain, and I should consider whether Russia's offer is genuine or a trap to get into BUL themselves. Russia moving to BUL/EC would actually contest BUL too unless they're supporting my move from GRE. Let me think carefully: if Russia moves F RUM → BUL/EC and I move A GRE → BUL with A CON supporting, that's actually a conflict — we'd both be trying to enter BUL. Russia's "help" could be a ploy to take BUL themselves. I should tell Russia to stay out of BUL and instead support my move from GRE. I need to warn Austria strongly that if they take BUL, I'll support Russia into RUM or take other hostile action.

MESSAGES:
{
  "AUSTRIA": "If A SER moves to BUL, we collide and both lose the center — that's a terrible outcome when BUL is uncontested otherwise. I'm moving A GRE to BUL this spring with support; please hold A SER or move it elsewhere. If you take BUL instead of me, I will have no choice but to work with Russia against Austrian positions in SER and TRI.",
  "RUSSIA": "I appreciate the offer, but F RUM moving to BUL/EC would conflict with my A GRE moving to BUL — we'd bounce each other. If you want to help, support my move by holding F RUM in place and keeping BUL/EC clear. I'm committed to no moves toward SEV or RUM this spring; let's coordinate cleanly rather than collide."
}

Looking at the current situation: Austria wants A SER - BUL, which would directly conflict with my A GRE - BUL. I need to be firm with Austria that I'm taking BUL and they should not move there. Russia offered F RUM - BUL/EC which I already pushed back on. Italy is moving F TUN - ION which is fine. I need to make clear final commitments and warn Austria of consequences if they try to take BUL.

MESSAGES:
{
  "AUSTRIA": "Final commitment: A GRE - BUL this spring, supported by A CON and F BLA. If A SER also moves to BUL, we both bounce and Russia keeps the center — a terrible outcome when it's free. Hold A SER in place and I commit A BUL will never threaten SER; that's a stable border that lets us both focus north on Russia's 6 SCs.",
  "RUSSIA": "Final commitment: no moves toward RUM or SEV this spring — A GRE - BUL, F BLA supporting, A CON supporting. Hold F RUM in place and keep BUL/EC clear so we don't accidentally bounce each other. This keeps our arrangement clean and BUL falls to me without any conflict."
}

England has committed to moving toward DEN/BEL while I push A BUR toward MUN, so I'll honor that coordination by moving A BUR to MUN to pressure Germany's heartland, advancing A POR to SPA to claim that supply center, and using my Atlantic fleets to support the push and maintain positional strength.

MESSAGES:
{
  "ENGLAND": "Confirming our deal: I'm pushing A BUR toward MUN this spring. Please follow through on moving toward DEN or BEL — if we both commit, Germany is squeezed from two sides and can't hold everything. Let's make this count.",
  "GERMANY": "I want to be honest with you: I'm under pressure to coordinate against you from England, and I've agreed to push A BUR toward MUN this spring. I'd rather have a deal with you than a war, but I need to know you're not planning to move against me either. If you can signal your intentions clearly, there may still be room to de-escalate."
}

Looking at the board: Germany just told me F BEL is moving to HOL, which opens BEL as a target. England is pressing DEN. If Germany pulls F BEL back to HOL, that's a huge opportunity — BEL becomes undefended and I could push A BUR toward BEL while England takes DEN. That would give me a 6th center and put Germany in serious trouble. I need to coordinate with England on this and make sure they're aware BEL is now open. I should also confirm with Germany that I'm not moving on RUH so they don't reverse the BEL move.

MESSAGES:
{
  "ENGLAND": "Breaking news: Germany just told me F BEL is moving to HOL this spring — that leaves BEL completely open. If you take DEN and I move A BUR into BEL, we each grab a center and Germany collapses to 2. I'm committing A BUR to BEL this turn. Can you confirm you're taking DEN so Germany can't reinforce BEL from the north?",
  "GERMANY": "I hear you on keeping the border quiet, and I appreciate F BEL moving to HOL — that's a real gesture. To be direct: if F BEL vacates BEL, I have a strong incentive to move A BUR there. If you want me to hold A BUR back, I need something concrete in return — what can you offer that makes holding more valuable than moving?"
}

Looking at the board: Germany proposed A MUN → BUR this fall, which would be a direct attack on my position. England suggested I take BEL while they take HOL. The cleanest path forward is A BUR → BEL (Germany's F BEL can't support HOL if it's defending itself), and England takes HOL with F NTH. I need to confirm England is actually doing this and warn Germany that attacking BUR is suicidal.

MESSAGES:
{
  "ENGLAND": "Here's the clean deal: I move A BUR → BEL this fall, you move F NTH → HOL. F BEL cannot simultaneously defend itself and support HOL — we each take a center guaranteed. I'm committing to A BUR → BEL if you confirm F NTH → HOL right now. Yes or no?",
  "GERMANY": "I heard you're considering A MUN → BUR — that would be a catastrophic mistake. A PAR sits right behind BUR and I have support available; you'd be walking into a trap while England takes HOL uncontested. Hold A MUN and we can still talk, but if you attack BUR I will take MUN with everything I have next spring."
}

Looking at the board: Austria wants me to support A SER - GRE with F ION, but Austria already has 5 centers and is growing fast — if GRE goes to Austria rather than Italy, that's a missed opportunity for me. Turkey wants me to stay passive. I need to push for GRE going to Italy (F ION - GRE) rather than supporting Austria's A SER - GRE. The key question is whether Austria will support my F ION into GRE or insist on taking it themselves with A SER.

MESSAGES:
{
  "AUSTRIA": "I'm committed to moving F ION - GRE this fall, not supporting your A SER - GRE — GRE was my center and I need it back, not you. If you support F ION - GRE with F ALB or A SER, we take it cleanly and I gain the center; in exchange I'll give you solid support on your Adriatic/Balkans position with A VEN. If you move A SER - GRE instead, we likely bounce each other and Turkey keeps GRE — that's the worst outcome for both of us.",
  "TURKEY": "I'm moving F ION toward GRE this fall — Austria has A SER and F ALB threatening from the north, and I can't hold back any longer. Your best move is to withdraw A BUL to a defensive position and commit F AEG to hold, so you don't lose both GRE and get squeezed from two sides simultaneously; if you concede GRE to me now, I'll keep F ION out of AEG and we have a real boundary to work from."
}

Looking at the situation: England has confirmed F NTH → HOL with F DEN support, and I've committed A BUR → BEL. This is the cleanest coordination we've had — F BEL cannot both defend itself against A BUR AND support HOL simultaneously. Germany is trying to talk me out of it with vague promises about Russia. I should lock this in with England and ignore Germany's stalling.

MESSAGES:
{
  "ENGLAND": "Locked: A BUR → BEL, A PAR supporting A BUR → BEL for extra strength. You move F NTH → HOL with F DEN support — F BEL cannot defend and support simultaneously, we each take a center. This is the cleanest execution we've had; I'm not pivoting this time.",
  "GERMANY": "Your proposal to hold in place is exactly what you've said every season while Russia grows and you stall me — I'm not holding A BUR again. The only way to stop this is to move F BEL out of the way now and offer me something concrete, not vague future promises."
}

Looking at the situation: Spring 1906 bounced because England supported DEN instead of attacking HOL, and F BEL held freely. I've committed to A BUR → BEL with A PAR support again this fall, which is the right call. The key question is whether England actually executes F NTH → HOL with F DEN support this time, or stalls again. I should also consider whether Germany might vacate BEL voluntarily to preserve their other centers.

MESSAGES:
{
  "ENGLAND": "I'm locked: A BUR → BEL supported by A PAR — two units against F BEL, it cannot hold. You must move F NTH → HOL with F DEN supporting it, not protecting DEN. If you stall again and F BEL holds freely, I'm moving F MAO → ENG next spring and treating you as the obstacle. Confirm F NTH → HOL + F DEN support explicitly.",
  "GERMANY": "Two French units are hitting BEL this fall — F BEL cannot hold against A BUR + A PAR support. Your only play is to retreat F BEL voluntarily to HOL or RUH now, preserve that unit, and we negotiate a ceasefire before you lose it outright. A voluntary move saves the fleet; a forced retreat wastes it."
}

Looking at the board: England just proposed F NTH → HOL with F DEN support (2 units) simultaneously with my A BEL → HOL + A BUR support (2 units). That would bounce both of us out of HOL — neither gets the center. I need to either get England to commit to NOT moving into HOL (just supporting or holding), or pivot to a different target. England's message is suspicious — they're proposing a simultaneous attack that would bounce, not a coordinated one where I take HOL. I should call this out and demand a cleaner arrangement, while also considering A BEL → RUH as an alternative if England won't commit cleanly.

MESSAGES:
{
  "ENGLAND": "Your proposal has F NTH → HOL AND A BEL → HOL simultaneously — that's a bounce, not a capture. If we both move into HOL, neither of us gets it and Germany keeps the center free. Concrete demand: either you hold F NTH in place (or move F DEN → HEL to cut support) while I take A BEL → HOL with A BUR support, or I pivot A BEL → RUH this fall instead. Which is it — do you want HOL to fall to me, or are you trying to bounce us both?",
  "GERMANY": "England just proposed moving F NTH → HOL with F DEN support simultaneously with my A BEL → HOL — they're trying to take HOL themselves, not coordinate with me. If England takes HOL, that's 6 centers for them versus your 4 and my 6 — England becomes the dominant power. I'm genuinely considering pivoting A BEL → RUH this fall to squeeze your interior instead of gifting HOL to England; a voluntary F HOL → KIE still gives you the cleanest exit before England's fleet overwhelms the north."
}

Looking at the board: France confirmed they're taking BEL back from Germany (A HOL → BEL + A BUR support), which means France gets BEL and reaches 7 centers. Germany's F KIE is exposed to my F HEL + F DEN at 2v1 if A BER and A MUN don't support it. I should confirm with France that I'm taking KIE so we don't conflict, and lock in my plan to press KIE this fall.

MESSAGES:
{
  "FRANCE": "Confirmed: I'm pressing F HEL → KIE with F DEN support this fall — that's a clean 2v1 against Germany's F KIE unless A BER or A MUN defends it. You retake BEL with A HOL + A BUR, I take KIE — Germany collapses to 2 centers and we each gain one. Are you committing A HOL → BEL + A BUR support? Confirm and I lock KIE.",
  "GERMANY": "France is taking BEL back with A HOL + A BUR at 2v1 — that's confirmed. Your only viable move is to pull F KIE somewhere useful and accept BEL is gone, or try to hold BEL and lose KIE to my F HEL + F DEN instead. Cede KIE to me and I'll actively block France from pressing further into your territory next year."
}

Looking at the board: Germany's F BEL is in my supply center and I need it back. A HOL → BEL with A BUR support is a clean 2-on-1. England wants KIE — I should confirm coordination so we don't interfere. Germany is trying to scare me about HOL being taken back, but with A BUR supporting the HOL→BEL move, Germany can't counter-support effectively. I need to nail down England's commitment and warn Germany that resistance is futile.

MESSAGES:
{
  "ENGLAND": "Confirmed: A HOL → BEL with A BUR support this fall — that's a 2-on-1 dislodge of F BEL regardless of what Germany does. You press F HEL → KIE or F DEN → KIE simultaneously and Germany collapses to 2 centers; we can divide MUN and KIE between us next spring. Lock KIE and I lock BEL — confirm.",
  "GERMANY": "Germany, F BEL → KIE is exactly what I want you to do — it means you're conceding BEL voluntarily and I walk in unopposed. If you stay in BEL, A HOL + A BUR dislodges you 2-on-1 and you lose the unit with nowhere clean to retreat. Your best play is F BEL → KIE now and we discuss MUN separately."
}

Looking at the board: I have F GRE, A VEN, F TYS, A NAP. I need a 5th center. TUN is unoccupied and reachable via F TYS - TUN. F GRE can support Austria's A BUL if Austria is pressing RUM, or I can use it defensively. A VEN could press TRI or TYR. The key question is whether Austria is genuinely moving A BUL - RUM (away from GRE) or whether they might turn on GRE. Austria is at 5 centers and moving A BUL away from GRE would be good for me. TUN gives me a clean 5th center. I should secure that and coordinate with Austria against Russia.

MESSAGES:
{
  "AUSTRIA": "I'm committing F TYS - TUN this fall to take my 5th center — that's a clean gain with no conflict between us. If you're pressing A BUL - RUM, I can hold F GRE defensively to cover your southern flank or support the push; tell me which you need. Russia at 7 centers is the threat we both need to address.",
  "TURKEY": "I'm not pressing further against you this fall — F TYS is moving to TUN and F GRE holds. If you want to coordinate F ION toward something useful against Russia or Austria, I'm listening; a surviving Turkey with fleet presence in ION is more valuable to me as a buffer than as a target."
}
